Here is the refactored code using Clean Architecture and Ports & Adapters:

```typescript
// Port interface for external services
interface IExternalService {
  getCountryByIp(ip: string): Promise<string>;
}

// Adapter class to implement the port interface
class ExternalGeolocationAdapter implements IExternalService {
  async getCountryByIp(ip: string): Promise<string> {
    const res = await fetch(`https://ipapi.co/${ip}/json/`);
    const data = await res.json();
    return data.country_name;
  }
}

// Use case class to encapsulate the business logic
class GetCountryUseCase {
  constructor(private readonly externalService: IExternalService) {}

  async execute(ip: string): Promise<string> {
    return this.externalService.getCountryByIp(ip);
  }
}

// Repository interface for data access
interface IRepository {
  getCountryByIp(ip: string): Promise<string>;
}

// Data access class to interact with the database or other data source
class GeolocationRepository implements IRepository {
  async getCountryByIp(ip: string): Promise<string> {
    // Implement your logic here, e.g., fetch from a database
    return 'United States';
  }
}

// Service class that uses the use case and repository
class GeolocationService {
  constructor(private readonly useCase: GetCountryUseCase, private readonly repository: IRepository) {}

  async getCountryByIp(ip: string): Promise<string> {
    try {
      const country = await this.useCase.execute(ip);
      return country;
    } catch (error) {
      console.error('Error fetching country:', error);
      throw new Error('Failed to fetch country');
    }
  }
}

// Main entry point
async function main() {
  const externalService = new ExternalGeolocationAdapter();
  const repository = new GeolocationRepository();
  const useCase = new GetCountryUseCase(externalService, repository);
  const service = new GeolocationService(useCase, repository);

  try {
    const country = await service.getCountryByIp('8.8.8.8');
    console.log(`The country for IP address '8.8.8.8' is ${country}`);
  } catch (error) {
    console.error(error);
  }
}

main();
```

In this refactored code, we have separated the concerns of fetching data from an external service and storing it in a repository into different classes. The `GeolocationService` class uses the `GetCountryUseCase` to execute the business logic and interact with the `GeolocationRepository`. This adheres to Clean Architecture principles by separating the application's core components (use cases, repositories) from the infrastructure components (external services).

